WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.090
DINA PORTMAN: Hey, Martin,
what are you wearing?

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.740
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh hi, Dina.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.930
This is from an
e-commerce startup.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.240
Their dev team is
evaluating Cloud Run

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.610
for nine different
types of workloads.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.420
I told them we'd find
the answers for them.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.180
DINA PORTMAN: Sounds great.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.980
Stay tuned if you
want to know more.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.354
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.720
MARTIN OMANDER: Artizanship
connects rural artisans

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.550
with consumers worldwide.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.980
My new hat came from Ecuador.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.410
Their dev team is
excited about Cloud Run,

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.170
but they aren't sure if
it's a good fit for them.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.245
Here, I have a list of
nine types of workloads.

00:00:42.245 --> 00:00:44.120
Dina, could you tell me
if Cloud Run would be

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.620
a good fit for those workloads?

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.765
DINA PORTMAN: Sure,
I'll do my best.

00:00:48.765 --> 00:00:49.640
What's the first one?

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.890
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
they want to build

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.230
private microservices
that communicate

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.330
with each other asynchronously.

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:58.970
DINA PORTMAN: Cloud Run
would be a great fit.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.660
It would scale each service
up and down, independently

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.310
and automatically.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:07.610
The services would communicate
securely with each other

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.230
through Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:14.180
And the service endpoints would
be secured by authentication.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:19.190
If they prefer services to
explicitly call other services,

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:22.760
they should use Cloud
Tasks instead of Pub/Sub.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.610
The calls are still
robust and asynchronous,

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.610
but developers get more control
over things like retries,

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.360
scheduling, and rate limits.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.620
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
workload number two,

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.680
a public REST API using HTTPS.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.300
DINA PORTMAN: Oh, easy.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.530
They go to REST Service in Cloud
Run which provides an HTTPS

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.020
endpoint out of the box.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.285
They can also add their own
custom domain on this endpoint.

00:01:48.285 --> 00:01:51.110
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
workload number three,

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.180
scheduled maintenance jobs.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.700
DINA PORTMAN: They can trigger
their Cloud Run services

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.530
from Cloud Scheduler.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.960
Cloud Scheduler is
also serverless,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.230
and it uses the standard
Unix cron format.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:06.710
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
workload number four.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:11.270
They get data feeds from
suppliers in different formats.

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:15.135
The data needs to be transformed
and loaded into their database.

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:17.960
DINA PORTMAN: It's easy to
build ETL services in Cloud Run

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.590
and trigger them when files
are uploaded to Cloud Storage.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.530
Another alternative would be
to let Cloud Data Flow send

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.480
each record to
their Cloud Run ETL

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.580
service as a Pub/Sub message.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.850
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
workload number five.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.890
Oh, they need a public
website, of course.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.680
DINA PORTMAN: Well, they can
run a web framework, like Flask

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.990
or Express, on Cloud Run.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.360
If their website runs
on a software package,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.060
they may be able
to use Cloud Run.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.790
WordPress, for example,
works on Cloud Run,

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:49.310
but it requires
extra configuration.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.710
The team should also
check out App Engine,

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:55.970
which is a mature product for
building scalable web apps.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.670
Many content management
systems are also

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.100
available in the
GCP Marketplace.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.600
From the Marketplace,
you can deploy

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.820
production-grade solutions
to Compute Engine

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.950
with just a few clicks.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:11.970
There are images for WordPress,
Magento, Joomla, Drupal, et

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:12.685
cetera.

00:03:12.685 --> 00:03:16.060
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
workload number six.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.220
Oh, one of their microservices
create product images

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.990
using a binary library.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.480
DINA PORTMAN: Oh,
Cloud Run can do that.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.060
They would package up the
binary in a Docker container

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:27.230
and call it from the code.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:30.590
MARTIN OMANDER: Very well,
workload number seven.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:33.680
They also render video files
of some of their products.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.780
These jobs can
take hours to run.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.250
DINA PORTMAN: Well,
a Cloud Run job

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:41.000
can run for a maximum
of 60 minutes.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.030
Some long-running jobs can
be split into shorter jobs,

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:48.120
and this may simplify the code
and enable parallel processing.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.930
But if their jobs
can't be split up

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.000
and if they take
more than 60 minutes,

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.020
the team should use Compute
Engine or Kubernetes Engine.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.710
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
workload number eight,

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:04.160
off-the-shelf software
package for their finances

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.510
and warehouse management.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.550
DINA PORTMAN: Any
software or binary

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.170
that can run in
a Linux container

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:13.640
and does not store data on
disk can run in Cloud Run.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.860
If their finance or
warehouse software

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:18.829
can't run in a Linux
container, they

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:21.244
can run it on Compute
Engine, instead.

00:04:21.244 --> 00:04:23.420
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
workload number nine,

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:24.650
the final one.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.110
This is a product
recommendation engine

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.270
that will use machine learning.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.430
DINA PORTMAN: They should
use Recommendations AI

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.580
or another product in
Google's IA platform.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:36.740
But a Cloud Run
service can still

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.010
call those services to get
recommendations or even run

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:41.450
custom machine learning models.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:44.720
MARTIN OMANDER: Well, those
are all nine services.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.850
You seem awfully
well-prepared, Dina.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:48.815
Did you peek at my list?

00:04:48.815 --> 00:04:53.020
DINA PORTMAN: Well,
perhaps, but only a little.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:57.290
MARTIN OMANDER: [LAUGHS] We
hope you enjoyed this episode.

00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:00.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

